  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1206 AM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nGradual Warming Through The Week:\n\nAnticyclonic mid/upper level flow over the Rocky Mountain West \nadvecting east through the start of the week will cause a \ngradual warming trend through the Upper Mississippi River \nValley. Long range global ensemble probabilities for 576+ dam \ngeopotential heights at 500 mb progress the axis of 100% \nprobabilities across the Mississippi River Valley Tuesday night \nthrough Wednesday morning. Degree of warming/drying remain the \nforecast challenge with current NBM forecasting for mid 50/high \n30 temperatures/dewpoints through Friday. \n\nLimited Precipitation Potential Thursday:\n\nPassage of aforementioned synoptic ridge axis results in \nformation of a Rocky Mountain Low through midweek, subsequently \nadvecting northeast through the Dakotas and the Northwest Angle \nof Minnesota by Thursday morning. LREF consensus for this low \nlocation has increased over previous runs, limiting and \ndecreasing local precipitation probabilities through midweek. \nLREF probabilities for 0.1\" in 24 hours are nil in the most \nrecent runs (16.00Z) with probabilities for 0.01\"+ as high as \n50%/40% in the EPS/GEFS on Thursday.\n\nWeekend Precipitation Potential:\n\nAdditional Rocky Mountain Low cyclogenesis through the end of \nthe work week increases local precipitation potential as a \nTropical low undercuts the blocking anticyclonic mid level flow,\nultimately allowing a more zonal synoptic pattern across the \nUpper Midwest. Disagreement on formation and advection of the \nTropical Low affecting the western periphery anticyclonic flow \nbetween deterministic and ensemble guidance causes slight \ndiscrepancies eastern extent and timing of precipitation \naffecting the local forecast area. \n\nSimilar to recent forecast trends, highest LREF probabilities \n(0.1\" QPF / 24hrs) shunted north/northwest of the Upper \nMississippi River Valley until Saturday when an elongated \nfrontal boundary may be the source of supplementary cyclogenesis\nwherever the baroclinic boundary resides. Furthermore, \ndegradation of the mid level synoptic forcing due to a slowing \npattern increases dependence on a longer wave trough sagging \nsoutheast through southern Canada ultimately through the \nweekend. Therefore, while current NBM precipitation potential \nremains similar to previous forecasts, there is the potential \nfor an initially drier forecast to shake out for most of the \nlocal forecast area, outside of far western peripheral counties \nlocally in southeast Minnesota.",
